ers to grow this crop for the European dye trade, sparking the 1859 revolt and later leading Gandhi to Champaran in 1917 for his first satyagraha.", "name": "Indigo", "clue1": "A dye helped start a movement against the British Raj in Colonial India."}, {"id": "Mummy Brown", "hex": "#824B27", "hint": "Art suppliers sourced Egyptian mummies specifically to grind their wrappings, resin, and tissue into pigment for oil painting.", "name": "Mummy Brown", "clue1": "From the 1500s to the early 1900s, this paint was made by grinding up actual embalmed human remains."}], "points": 100, "prompt": "This is a prompt", "feedback": {"correct": "Great Eye!", "incorrect": "Not quite — try again and watch for the small details."}, "question": "Colors and their Stories", "attemptsAllowed": 3}]', 1, 'cmvnt2f04000c07l8bujp31ps', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-25 12:30:06.196', '2026-10-25 12:30:06.196');
INSERT INTO public.game (id, type, title, subtitle, "isPremium", "subStages", "schemaVersion", "importBatchId", "editionId", "position", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvnt1agq000107l880r4tfo3', 'fill-blank-text', 'Sufism', '', false, '[{"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Prithviraj Chauhan"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "In 1192, {{b1}}, the legendary Rajput warrior, was defeated by Muizuddin Muhammad bin Sam at the Second Battle of Tarain, opening India to Islamic mysticism. Four years later, Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti arrived from Afghanistan and established the Chishti Order, a spiritual movement that would transform Indian spirituality for the next 800 years.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}, {"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Ajmer"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "{{b1}}, Rajasthan, became the heart of Indian Sufism after Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti settled there in 1196, establishing Islam’s most visited shrine, which attracts 20,000+ pilgrims daily and 400,000+ during the annual Urs in Rajab, the 7th Islamic month. Its most sacred ceremony, Chhati Sharif, held on the 6th day, the recitation of the Chishti Order’s genealogical tree and concludes with a 21 gun salute.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}, {"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Basant Panchami"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "Delhi''s Nizamuddin Dargah celebrates {{b1}}, the 5th day of the Hindu lunar month Phalgun, marking spring''s arrival. Devotees drape the dargah in yellow flowers, symbolizing renewal and the Sufi concept of \"noor\" (divine light). This Hindu-Islamic fusion, 700+ years old, shows how Sufism wove spirituality across religious boundaries.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}, {"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Amir Khusrow"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "In 1244–1325, {{b1}} became one of Sufism’s greatest voices, was known as the “Nightingale of India.\" Though his master Hazrat Nizamuddin Auliya had 600+ khalifas (authorized disciples), he alone was declared his most beloved. The irony was profound: despite Nizamuddin’s view that musical instruments were haram (forbidden), his beloved disciple is credited with inventing the musical instrument and creating Qawwali, the musical form that made Sufism globally famous.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}, {"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Tabla"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "According to legend, he split the traditional pakhawaj (barrel drum) into two drums for Sufi music one the high-pitched dayan and deep bass bayan, creating the {{b1}}. Another tale claims Mughal musician Tansen''s accompanist''s pakhawaj cracked during performance, and Tansen exclaimed \"Tab bhi bola!\" (It still sounds!) supposedly naming the instrument from that moment.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}, {"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Whirling"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "The Turkish Mevlevi Order expressed spiritual devotion through {{b1}}, a ritual association with Rumi that was proclaimed a UNESCO Masterpiece of Oral and Intangible Heritage in 2005. The Arabic word samāʿ means \"listening\" or \"hearing\", making this movement a physical expression of spiritual listening through music, poetry, and prayer.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}, {"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Rabia"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "{{b1}} (the First Woman Sufi Saint), born into 8th-century Basra poverty, her name meaning ''fourth daughter'' became history''s first feminist mystic. Orphaned and enslaved, she prayed all night until her master witnessed divine light surrounding her. She rejected all marriage proposals, declaring marriage would distract her from God, act of radical autonomy in 8th-century Arabia.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}, {"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Langar"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "{{b1}}, a Persian institution within the Chishti Order, gives food and drink to the needy regardless of background. Sugar represents piety''s sweetness; salt symbolizes purity; bread''s transformation mirrors Sufi spiritual development and nafs (ego) purification.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}, {"blanks": [{"id": "b1", "answer": "Calligraphy"}], "points": 10, "prompt": "Sufi {{b1}} was an act of worship, not decoration. Each letter of Allah''s name was traced with spiritual intention, making the scribe a mystic. \"Tezhip\" (gilding) the golden illuminations in Qur''an manuscripts—served as spiritual technology to lift consciousness toward Paradise. Ink, paper, and brush became meditation tools as powerful as prayer. \nThrough saints and musicians, food and flowers, ink and rhythm, Sufism transformed from a response to political upheaval into a comprehensive spiritual path. The divine is reached through listening, service, love, and surrender of the self—the greatest transformation of all.", "feedback": {"correct": "Yay! You know this topic very well.", "incorrect": "No worries!! You can learn more about this at the end of this session."}, "question": "Fill in the missing words", "attemptsAllowed": 1}]', 1, 'cmvnt1ag1000007l8key3puvj', 'cmvrsrsa9000007jph9kynl4q', 0, '2026-10-25 12:29:13.633', '2026-10-28 07:33:51.471');
INSERT INTO public.game (id, type, title, subtitle, "isPremium", "subStages", "schemaVersion", "importBatchId", "editionId", "position", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvnt2y0j000h07l8mzi4a5ri', 'timeline-builder', 'Salt Through Time', 'Restore the Timeline', false, '[{"events": [{"id": "romania", "year": "6000 BC", "order": 1, "title": "Romanian Salt Springs", "description": "People living in what is now Romania boiled water from natural salt springs to collect salt. This is one of the oldest known salt-making sites in the world."}, {"id": "bulgaria", "year": "5500–4200 BC", "order": 2, "title": "Solnitsata Salt Center", "description": "At Solnitsata in present-day Bulgaria, people produced salt by boiling brine. The settlement later became one of Europe''s earliest known towns."}, {"id": "egypt-tombs", "year": "3000–2000 BC", "order": 3, "title": "Salt in Egyptian Tombs", "description": "Ancient Egyptians placed salt, salted birds and fish inside tombs as valuable funeral offerings."}, {"id": "egypt-trade", "year": "2800 BC", "order": 4, "title": "Egyptian Salt Trade", "description": "Egypt exported salted fish to the Phoenicians in exchange for cedar wood, glass and the famous Tyrian purple dye."}], "points": 100, "question": "Put the events in chronological order", "instructions": "Drag each historical event to its correct place on the timeline.", "estimatedTime": "3 mins", "attemptsAllowed": 3}, {"events": [{"id": "all-your-base", "year": "2001", "order": 1, "title": "All Your Base Are Belong to Us", "description": "A poorly translated cutscene from the game Zero Wing spreads across the early internet, becoming one of the first major viral memes."}, {"id": "rickroll", "year": "2007", "order": 2, "title": "Rickrolling Takes Off", "description": "The bait-and-switch prank of linking to Rick Astley’s ‘Never Gonna Give You Up’ becomes a massive internet phenomenon."}, {"id": "doge", "year": "2013", "order": 3, "title": "Doge Meme Rises", "description": "The Shiba Inu photo paired with broken English captions like ‘such wow’ and ‘very meme’ explodes in popularity."}, {"id": "distracted-boyfriend", "year": "2017", "order": 4, "title": "Distracted Boyfriend Goes Viral", "description": "A stock photo of a man checking out another woman while walking with his girlfriend becomes one of the most widely used meme templates."}], "points": 100, "question": "Put the memes in chronological order", "instructions": "Drag each meme event to its correct place on the timeline.", "estimatedTime": "3 mins", "attemptsAllowed": 3}]', 1, 'cmvnt2y04000g07l8cmu91jf7', NULL, NULL, '2026-10-25 12:30:30.82', '2026-10-25 12:30:30.82');
INSERT INTO public.game (id, type, title, subtitle, "isPremium", "subStages", "schemaVersion", "importBatchId", "editionId", "position", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES ('cmvp4zdjk000107lhkz46zreb', 'timeline-builder', 'Colours', 'Arrange the events in chronological order.', false, '[{"events": [{"id": "timeline-event-001", "year": "2500 BCE", "image": "img/f6fcc32da2e0-egyptian-blue.webp", "order": 1, "title": "Ancient Egypt''s Invention of Blue", "description": "Egyptian artisans heated sand, copper, and limestone together to create Egyptian Blue, considered the oldest known synthetic pigment in history. It was used for coloring the skin of gods and the beards of pharaohs'' statues to represent the heavens, the Nile, and rebirth."}, {"id": "timeline-event-002", "year": "1666", "image": "img/743d7ca2d6df-newton-prism.webp", "order": 2, "title": "Isaac Newton Discovers the Colours in White Light", "description": "Isaac Newton, working alone at Woolsthorpe Manor after Cambridge University closed due to plague, darkened his room, drilled a small hole in the shutter, and passed a beam of sunlight through a glass prism. He proved that white light is made of red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet combined."}, {"id": "timeline-event-003", "year": "1840", "image": "img/961f534c4899-queen-victoria-wedding.webp", "order": 3, "title": "The White Wedding Gown Tradition", "description": "Queen Victoria wore a plain white gown for her wedding to Prince Albert, instead of the elaborate, richly coloured robes expected of royalty. Newspapers described the white dress in detail, and brides across Britain began copying her choice, helping popularize the white wedding dress tradition."}, {"id": "timeline-event-004", "year": "1901–1904", "image": "img/9b2a6d2ba089-picasso-blue-period.webp", "order": 4, "title": "The Blue Period in Art", "description": "After the suicide of his close friend Carlos Casagemas, Pablo Picasso painted almost exclusively in shades of blue and blue-green for several years. Art historians now call this his Blue Period."}, {"id": "timeline-event-005", "year": "1978", "image": "img/54a16cb89f1d-gilbert-baker-rainbow-flag.webp", "order": 5, "title": "The Eight-Striped Rainbow Flag", "description": "Gilbert Baker, an artist and U.S. Army veteran living in San Francisco, hand-dyed and hand-sewed an eight-striped flag featuring hot pink, red, orange, yellow, green, turquoise, indigo, and violet, to fly at the San Francisco Gay Freedom Day Parade. Within about a year, the hot pink and turquoise stripes were dropped because the dyed fabric was too hard to source in bulk, leaving the six-colour rainbow flag recognised today."}, {"id": "timeline-event-006", "year": "2012", "image": "img/5c9177531b06-pink-blue-gender-colors.webp", "order": 6, "title": "Pink for Girls, Blue for Boys?", "description": "Psychologist Marco Del Giudice used Google''s book-scanning database to search millions of printed books for the phrases ''blue for boys'' and ''pink for girls.'' He found the pink-for-girls, blue-for-boys pairing had actually been consistent since the late 1800s, contradicting the popular claim that the colours were once reversed."}, {"id": "timeline-event-007", "year": "2015", "image": "img/62084f51a730-pink-cricket-ball.webp", "order": 7, "title": "Cricket''s First Day-Night Test with a Pink Ball", "description": "Australia and New Zealand played the first day-night Test match in cricket''s 138-year history, at Adelaide Oval. Since a traditional red ball is nearly invisible under floodlights, organisers used a specially coated pink ball instead, chosen after years of trials for staying visible against both grass and a darkening sky."}, {"id": "timeline-event-008", "year": "2016", "image": "img/eafa7ef10df1-unicorn-frappuccino.webp", "order": 8, "title": "The Millennial Pink Trend", "description": "Starbucks released the limited-edition Unicorn Frappuccino, a pink-and-blue layered drink, for a single weekend. It sold out at locations worldwide within hours and became one of the defining products of the ''millennial pink'' wave that had already been building across fashion, design, and Instagram since the term was first coined by New York Magazine."}], "points": 10, "question": "Arrange the events in chronological order.", "instructions": "Drag each historical event to its correct place on the timeline.", "estimatedTime": "5 mins", "attemptsAllowed": 1}]', 1, 'cmvp4zdj1000007lhu9a6t7tm', 'cmvqdexes000008l8o1kja56c', 0, '2026-10-26 10:51:25.885', '2026-10-27 07:35:23.594');


--
-- PostgreSQL database dump complete
--
---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Updating Edition dates as released in last 4 days + today + tomorrow
--
---------------------------------------------------------------------
BEGIN;

CREATE TEMP TABLE new_dates AS
WITH ranked AS (
  SELECT id,
         row_number() OVER (ORDER BY date) AS rn,
         count(*) OVER ()                  AS total
  FROM edition
  WHERE date IS NOT NULL
)
SELECT id,
       ((now() AT TIME ZONE 'Asia/Kolkata')::date + (rn + 1 - total)::int) AS new_date
FROM ranked
WHERE rn > total - 6;

UPDATE edition
SET date = NULL
WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM new_dates);

UPDATE edition e
SET date = n.new_date
FROM new_dates n
WHERE e.id = n.id;

SELECT e.title, e.date, e.status
FROM edition e
JOIN new_dates n ON n.id = e.id
ORDER BY e.date;

COMMIT;

